From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steelhead99x <steelhead99x@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:00:01 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(portable): Poly1305 in 26-bit limbs on 32-bit targets

The portable backend ran HACL*'s Poly1305 on 32-bit targets. Its limbs
are 26 bits, but libcrux keeps them in u64s, and a 32-bit core without
NEON spends most of the MAC in 64-bit multiplies and adds: on an
ESP32-S3 (Xtensa LX7, 240 MHz) a sampling profile of ChaCha20-Poly1305
put 58% of the time there, and rind sealed 16 KiB 1.27x slower than
ring, whose ChaCha20 it beats.

generic::poly1305_32 is poly1305-donna-32's radix and carry chain in
rind's style: five u32 limbs, each product one 32x32->64-bit multiply,
partial reduction per block, and a masked final subtraction of p. The
portable backend picks it where target_pointer_width = "32", and where
rind_limb = "32" is forced, so CI can test it on x86_64 under
--cfg rind_portable --cfg rind_limb="32". 64-bit targets keep
Graviola's 64-bit limbs; armv7 keeps NEON, and HACL*'s without it.
Unlike HACL*'s streaming state it counts no input, so it has no 2^32
byte limit.

Its correctness argument is the bounds in its comments (every limb sum
under 2^27 + 2^6, every product sum under 2^58, every carry under
2^32), checked by debug assertions, and its tests: RFC 8439 2.5.2 and
A.3 #5-#11, aws-lc's Poly1305 vectors, split updates, and agreement
with Graviola's and HACL*'s Poly1305 over every length to 300 bytes,
random keys and lengths to 16 KiB fed in random pieces (512 cases in an
optimised build, 96 in a debug one), and all-ones and all-zeros keys
and messages. The last marks key and message secret for admin/ctgrind.
Wycheproof's ChaCha20-Poly1305 and XChaCha20-Poly1305 vectors, aws-lc's
AEAD vectors and the crosschecks reach it under the forced cfgs.

It branches on no secret and indexes by none. Its time depends on no
secret where a 32x32->64-bit multiply takes the same time for every
operand, as on the ESP32-S3 and Cortex-M4; Cortex-M3's umull does not.

Co-authored-by: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
